{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposal creates a 'migration cliff' where the cost of exporting data from a disposable script to a formal tool is underestimated; if the script lacks a defined schema from day one, the accumulated data becomes unstructured noise that cannot be migrated without manual re-entry, effectively wasting the early research phase.","counterexample":["A team writes a quick CSV logger for Phase 1, but columns shift ad-hoc as hypotheses change. When Phase 2 begins, the 6 months of historical data cannot be mapped to the rigid schema of the off-the-shelf tool, forcing the team to discard all prior results or spend weeks manually cleaning them.","The 'disposable' script uses local file paths that break when the team attempts to move to a cloud-based tracker, requiring a complete rewrite of the data ingestion pipeline rather than a simple migration."],"premise":"Data accumulated in the script-based tracker cannot be easily exported or transformed.","premises_essential":["1a36c8265da994bb07de3d93ed583d987252c54734b21ee0f4f97971471095a3"],"successor_question":"What specific schema constraints must be enforced in the 'disposable' phase to guarantee low-cost migration later?","target":"1a36c8265da994bb07de3d93ed583d987252c54734b21ee0f4f97971471095a3"},{"attack":true,"case":"The argument assumes existing tools are purely additive to cognitive bandwidth, ignoring the 'integration tax': if no existing tool perfectly matches the novel workflow, the engineering effort required to hack, plugin, or wrap a mismatched tool can exceed the cost of building a minimal bespoke solution, while still imposing the tool's rigid mental model on the researchers.","counterexample":["A team adopts a popular general-purpose tracker but spends 40% of their sprint building custom adapters because the tool assumes a 'train/test' split workflow that doesn't exist in their continuous online learning research, slowing down innovation more than building a simple custom logger would have.","Researchers stop running certain types of experiments because the adopted tool makes recording those specific metrics cumbersome, thereby distorting the research direction to fit the tool rather than the science."],"premise":"No existing tool supports the specific data modality or workflow required by the research.","premises_essential":["47bf7d317219d1d2f5a85407937fc5c0e2e3be742aa482e684014d4ba1439fce"],"successor_question":"At what point does the friction of adapting a generalist tool outweigh the initial development cost of a specialist one?","target":"47bf7d317219d1d2f5a85407937fc5c0e2e3be742aa482e684014d4ba1439fce"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'skeptic validation layer' introduces a sequential bottleneck that contradicts the need for rapid iteration in early-stage research; requiring independent reproduction before treating results as 'provisional' (or accepted) effectively halts the feedback loop, causing the team to miss the 'survival threshold' mentioned in its own counterconditions.","counterexample":["A team generates 50 hypothesis variations a week; the validation layer requires re-running each twice before the next batch can be designed based on those results. This triples the time-to-insight, causing the team to run out of funding before finding a viable model.","The validation layer flags a promising anomaly as 'unreproduced,' causing the team to discard it as noise, only for a competitor using a faster, less rigorous loop to discover it was a breakthrough signal."],"premise":"The overhead of the validation layer slows down iteration beyond the team's survival threshold.","premises_essential":["56a44ca1e5c4aa4266afb7e4e41e35b76747722c563bef7026ea9975839cbe90"],"successor_question":"Can the validation layer operate asynchronously on historical data rather than blocking the forward experimental loop?","target":"56a44ca1e5c4aa4266afb7e4e41e35b76747722c563bef7026ea9975839cbe90"},{"attack":true,"case":"Configuring an existing tool to highlight negative results often clashes with the tool's underlying data model which is optimized for 'runs' and 'metrics' as positive signals; forcing equal prominence can render the dashboard unreadable due to signal dilution, making it impossible for stakeholders to identify actual progress or pivot points quickly.","counterexample":["A dashboard configured to show failed hypotheses with equal visual weight to successes becomes a wall of red errors, causing stakeholders to lose confidence in the project's viability despite a few key breakthroughs being buried in the noise.","The tool's filtering mechanisms are designed to surface top-k performance; overriding this to show 'all failures' overwhelms the UI, leading researchers to ignore the dashboard entirely and revert to informal communication channels."],"premise":"The volume of negative results overwhelms the signal, making the tracker unusable.","premises_essential":["9b44d6896584dfd1a67415125aa3cb9d693f74898a29b27e22ca31cf06b0fae1"],"successor_question":"What aggregation or summarization strategy can represent the *value* of negative results without displaying every single failure with equal visual prominence?","target":"9b44d6896584dfd1a67415125aa3cb9d693f74898a29b27e22ca31cf06b0fae1"}]}